    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d4">

                <pb facs="0039" n="7" sameAs="frus:frus1958-60v06/pg7"/>

                <head>4. Telegram From the Embassy in Cuba to the Department of State<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d4-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Havana</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1958-01-11T18:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">January 11,
                                1958—6 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>369. For Rubottom. Guell reports Felipe Pazos claims Department will cooperate
                    with opposition by holding back on delivery of arms to GOC.</p>
                <p>GOC anxious for prompt delivery of 20 armored cars. See previous cables. Batista
                    states he does not want to embarrass US. So if delivery is to be deferred he
                    will cancel order rather than suffer delay and request refund money on
                    deposit.</p>
                <p>Reference Embtel 368.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d4-f2"/></p>
                <p>Because I have obtained definite assurances from Batista that he will not
                    continue suspension guarantees after January 27, I urgently request that we make
                    delivery of armored cars on schedule. If GOC is forced to cancel this order
                    because of our deferring delivery, it may seriously damage my overall
                    objectives. Therefore I earnestly hope you will support me in this request.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Smith</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d4-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d4-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Central Files, 737.00/1–1158. Secret.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d4-f2" n="2" xml:id="d4-f2-content">
                <p>In telegram 368, January 11, Smith reported that he had obtained assurances that
                    day that Batista would restore constitutional guarantees on January 27, on
                    condition that no unforeseen violence would break out prior to that time. Smith
                    said he obtained this promise without offering any U.S. Government commitments,
                    only his “personal assurance” that the Cuban Government could rely on the full
                    cooperation of the United States. (<hi rendition="#italic">Ibid</hi>.)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
